# .env.dev — the one place the local development variable set is stated.
#
# HOW IT IS USED
#   Every `make run-*` target sources this file with PP_SERVICE_NAME already set:
#
#       set -a; PP_SERVICE_NAME=payment-api; . ./.env.dev; set +a; go run ./cmd/payment-api
#
#   `set -a` exports everything assigned below, so the process sees them without each
#   assignment needing its own `export`. Sourcing it by hand works the same way, which is
#   what makes `go run ./cmd/payment-api` debuggable outside make.
#
# WHY A FILE RATHER THAN VARIABLES IN THE MAKEFILE
#   Nine binaries share one variable set with a handful of per-service differences. Stated
#   nine times in a Makefile, the set drifts: one target gets a new required variable and the
#   other eight do not, and the failure is a crash-looping process with a message nobody reads
#   until they try that particular target. Stated once, adding a required variable is one edit.
#
# WHY THESE VALUES AND NOT config/dev.yaml's
#   config/dev.yaml documents the shape of a configuration; nothing loads it (see
#   config/README.md). The runtime is twelve-factor: it reads PP_* from the environment, and
#   `internal/platform/runtime` refuses to start when a `required:"true"` variable is missing.
#   The values here are kept in step with config/dev.yaml by hand, and where they differ the
#   reason is stated inline.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS A SECRET
#   The database password is the one deploy/docker-compose.dev.yml creates, and it is spelled
#   so that it reads as a placeholder to a human and to scripts/check-secrets.sh's S2 rule
#   alike. Every real credential is a `secret://` reference resolved at the moment of use.
#   This file is committed on purpose; `.gitignore` carries an explicit exception for it.

# --- identity -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PP_ENVIRONMENT is required and has exactly two legal values. `development` is NOT one of
# them: runtime.ParseEnvironment accepts `sandbox` and `production` and nothing else, and a
# process handed anything else exits before it binds a port. `sandbox` is also what permits
# the gateway simulator, which the composition roots refuse outside it.
PP_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox
PP_REGION=local
PP_LOG_LEVEL=debug

# --- telemetry ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Full sampling: sampling exists to bound cost at production volume, and at development volume
# there is no cost to bound. Empty OTLP endpoint means export is disabled, which is correct for
# a developer with no collector — set it to localhost:4317 when `make dev-up` is running.
PP_TRACE_SAMPLE_RATIO=1.0
PP_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=${PP_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT:-}
PP_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_INSECURE=true

# --- postgres -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The runtime reads PP_DATABASE_URL. PP_DSN is what `platformctl` and scripts/migrate.sh read,
# and the two are deliberately both set here to the same value: a developer who sets one and
# not the other gets a service that starts against a database the migrations never touched.
PP_DATABASE_URL=${PP_DATABASE_URL:-postgres://pp_dev:pp_dev_not_a_real_password@localhost:5432/pp?sslmode=disable}
PP_DSN=${PP_DSN:-$PP_DATABASE_URL}
PP_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS=5
PP_DATABASE_MIN_CONNS=1
PP_DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=5s
PP_DATABASE_LOCK_TIMEOUT=2s

# --- redis --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE, AND IT IS A DEFECT REPORT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
#
# `internal/infrastructure/redis` refuses TLS-less Redis unless the environment is one of
# `local`, `test`, `development` or `dev` (Config.isLocal), while `runtime.ParseEnvironment`
# accepts only `sandbox` and `production`. The two sets do not intersect, so there is no
# startable configuration in which a plaintext local Redis is permitted:
#
#   PP_REDIS_TLS=false  ->  "REDIS_TLS: TLS may not be disabled outside a local environment"
#   PP_REDIS_TLS=true   ->  every operation fails a TLS handshake against a plaintext container
#
# An empty PP_REDIS_ADDR is a supported configuration and the honest one meanwhile:
# runtime.OpenRedis returns (nil, nil), idempotency falls back to Postgres — which is
# authoritative anyway (ADR-009) — and rate limiting falls back to the local token bucket.
# The cost is ~15-30 ms of p99, exactly as docs/runbooks/redis-loss.md describes.
#
# To use Redis locally, either run one with TLS and set the three variables below, or fix
# redis.Config.isLocal to accept `sandbox`. The second is the real fix.
PP_REDIS_ADDR=${PP_REDIS_ADDR:-}
PP_REDIS_TLS=${PP_REDIS_TLS:-true}
PP_REDIS_DB=0
PP_REDIS_POOL_SIZE=5

# --- kafka --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLAINTEXT is what deploy/docker-compose.dev.yml's Redpanda speaks, and it is what these
# values describe. BE AWARE OF TWO DEFECTS THAT CURRENTLY STOP `outbox-relay` AND
# `event-consumer` FROM STARTING LOCALLY. Neither is a mistake in this file:
#
#   1. `internal/infrastructure/kafka.Config.isLocal` permits PLAINTEXT and SASL_PLAINTEXT only
#      when the environment is `local`, `test`, `development` or `dev`, and
#      `runtime.ParseEnvironment` accepts only `sandbox` and `production`. The two sets do not
#      intersect, so PLAINTEXT is unreachable from any startable configuration.
#        -> "KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL: PLAINTEXT is only permitted in a local environment"
#
#   2. Switching to SSL or SASL_SSL does not help, and this one also affects production:
#      `Config.ClientOptions` sets both `kgo.Dialer` and `kgo.DialTLSConfig`, and franz-go
#      rejects that combination outright ("cannot set both Dialer and DialTLSConfig"), so
#      client construction fails for every TLS-using protocol.
#        -> "startup failed during kafka producer: kafka: creating producer client"
#
# The fixes belong in internal/infrastructure/kafka: accept `sandbox` in isLocal, and drop the
# plain `kgo.Dialer` when DialTLSConfig is set. Until then the six non-Kafka run-* targets work
# and these two do not, which is stated here rather than discovered at 09:00 on a Monday.
PP_KAFKA_BROKERS=${PP_KAFKA_BROKERS:-localhost:19092}
PP_KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=${PP_KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL:-PLAINTEXT}

# --- authentication -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These three are required with no defaults, deliberately: a defaulted issuer, audience or
# JWKS URL is an authentication control that appears configured and verifies nothing.
# They point at scripts/devissuer, which `make dev-up` and `make run-dev-issuer` start.
# The trailing slash on the issuer matters — `iss` is compared for exact string equality.
PP_AUTH_JWKS_URL=${PP_AUTH_JWKS_URL:-http://localhost:8088/.well-known/jwks.json}
PP_AUTH_ISSUER=${PP_AUTH_ISSUER:-http://localhost:8088/}
PP_AUTH_AUDIENCE=${PP_AUTH_AUDIENCE:-payments-platform-dev}
PP_AUTH_MAX_TOKEN_AGE=8h

# --- gateways -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Short timeout so a hang shows up immediately: a local simulator answers in microseconds, and
# an 8-second budget hides a hang for 8 seconds.
PP_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT=3s
PP_GATEWAY_ENABLE_SIMULATOR=true
PP_GATEWAY_SIMULATOR_URL=${PP_GATEWAY_SIMULATOR_URL:-http://localhost:8090}

# --- configuration snapshot -----------------------------------------------------------------
# A generous cliff: a developer who stops the control plane should still be able to drive
# payments against the last snapshot rather than watching everything fail closed.
PP_CONFIG_REFRESH_INTERVAL=5s
PP_CONFIG_MAX_STALENESS=30s
PP_CONFIG_CLIFF_STALENESS=30m

# --- secrets ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty backend selects `file` in sandbox and `aws` in production. An empty PP_SECRETS_FILE is
# a valid configuration: the provider comes up holding no material, which is what a developer
# wants until they need a gateway credential. `platformctl seed` prints the references to put
# in the document when they do.
PP_SECRETS_BACKEND=${PP_SECRETS_BACKEND:-}
PP_SECRETS_FILE=${PP_SECRETS_FILE:-}
PP_SECRETS_CACHE_TTL=60s

# --- shedding and workers -------------------------------------------------------------------
PP_CONCURRENCY_INITIAL_LIMIT=16
PP_CONCURRENCY_MIN_LIMIT=4
PP_CONCURRENCY_MAX_LIMIT=64
PP_WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL=500ms
PP_WORKER_BATCH_SIZE=20
PP_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- test-suite conveniences ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by tests/testenv. Exported here so `make run-*` and `make test-e2e` agree about where
# the stack is, rather than each developer remembering a different port.
PP_TEST_BASE_URL=${PP_TEST_BASE_URL:-http://localhost:8080}
PP_TEST_CONTROL_URL=${PP_TEST_CONTROL_URL:-http://localhost:8082}
PP_TEST_SIMULATOR_URL=${PP_TEST_SIMULATOR_URL:-http://localhost:8090}
PP_DEV_ISSUER_URL=${PP_DEV_ISSUER_URL:-http://localhost:8088}
# Persist the issuer's signing key across restarts. Without it the key is regenerated on
# every start — which is the right default for a throwaway process, and maddening in a dev
# loop: services cache the JWKS in the background (5 min refresh, 30 s negative cache), so a
# restarted issuer means every existing token fails with UNKNOWN_KEY until the next refresh.
# .dev/ is gitignored; the key is a local development artefact, not a credential.
PP_DEV_ISSUER_KEY_FILE=${PP_DEV_ISSUER_KEY_FILE:-.dev/issuer-key.pem}

# --- per-service settings ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sourced with PP_SERVICE_NAME already set, so one file covers nine binaries. Each binary
# embeds only the configuration groups it needs, so what it does not embed it does not require:
# outbox-relay has no HTTP listener and therefore no PP_HTTP_ADDR, and asking for one would be
# a variable an operator must set correctly for a port nothing binds.
#
# Ports are distinct across every service because `make run-*` runs them on one host. The
# admin listener carries /healthz, /readyz, /livez and /metrics on a separate port, so an
# ingress that routes the public port cannot reach /metrics by construction.
case "${PP_SERVICE_NAME:-}" in
  payment-api)
    PP_HTTP_ADDR=:8080
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8081
    PP_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
    PP_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
    PP_HSTS_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=0
    ;;
  payment-orchestrator)
    # Internal gRPC with mTLS workload identity, never public HTTP — so it carries the mesh
    # group and no HTTP group. PP_MESH_TRUST_DOMAIN is required: accepting any trust domain
    # means accepting a certificate from any mesh that can reach the port.
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8087
    PP_GRPC_ADDR=:9095
    PP_MESH_TRUST_DOMAIN=${PP_MESH_TRUST_DOMAIN:-local.payments.internal}
    PP_GRPC_REFLECTION=true
    ;;
  control-plane-api)
    PP_HTTP_ADDR=:8082
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8092
    PP_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8082
    PP_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
    # L2 policy inputs. Both required with no default: an empty supported list means "nowhere",
    # and defaulting it to "anywhere" would let a merchant be onboarded in a jurisdiction the
    # platform holds no licence for.
    PP_L2_SUPPORTED_COUNTRIES=DE,FR,GB,US,NL,ES,IT
    PP_L2_SANCTIONED_COUNTRIES=KP,IR,SY,CU
    PP_L2_MONTHLY_VOLUME_CEILING=100000000
    PP_L2_VOLUME_CEILING_CURRENCY=EUR
    ;;
  webhook-ingress)
    # No auth group: the caller is a gateway, which holds no platform credential and is
    # authenticated by its own signature scheme inside the handler.
    PP_HTTP_ADDR=:8083
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8093
    PP_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8083
    ;;
  workflow-worker)
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8084
    PP_WORKFLOW_NAME=merchant-onboarding
    # HeartbeatInterval*3 must not exceed LeaseDuration; the worker refuses to start otherwise,
    # because a heartbeat that races the expiry produces an instance claimed by two workers.
    PP_WORKFLOW_LEASE=60s
    PP_WORKFLOW_HEARTBEAT=15s
    ;;
  outbox-relay)
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8085
    PP_RELAY_SHARD=0
    PP_RELAY_TOTAL_SHARDS=1
    ;;
  event-consumer)
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8086
    # Both required with no default. A defaulted group means two deployments silently share
    # one and each sees half the partitions; empty topics means a process that reports healthy
    # and consumes nothing.
    PP_CONSUMER_GROUP=${PP_CONSUMER_GROUP:-pp-dev-projections}
    PP_CONSUMER_TOPICS=${PP_CONSUMER_TOPICS:-pp.payments.payment.v1,pp.merchants.merchant.v1,pp.config.configuration.v1}
    PP_CONSUMER_DEDUP_RETENTION=168h
    ;;
  gateway-simulator)
    # No Postgres and no Kafka: the simulator holds its state in memory on purpose.
    PP_HTTP_ADDR=:8090
    PP_ADMIN_ADDR=:8091
    # Required even here: an unauthenticated simulator on a shared network is an endpoint
    # anything can drive, and a test that can be driven by anything is a test whose failures
    # nobody can attribute. This is a local fixture value, not a credential.
    PP_SIMULATOR_API_KEY=${PP_SIMULATOR_API_KEY:-dev-simulator-not-a-real-key}
    ;;
  ''|*)
    # Sourced without a service name — a developer exploring, or a script that only wants the
    # shared set. Nothing service-specific is defined, and that is deliberate: guessing would
    # hand somebody payment-api's ports for a process that is not payment-api.
    :
    ;;
esac
